<DemoExample Title="Vertical" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example13CsharpCode" Id="example13" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        <b>Vertical</b> stacks the options in a column, which turns the navbar into the navigation rail that
        runs down the side of a page instead of across the foot of it. The header, the options and the
        footer all follow that direction, and a rail is normally sized with <b>FitWidth</b>, so it takes the
        width of its own content rather than the width of its container. Everything the bar does the rail
        does too: <b>InlineText</b> and <b>IconOnly</b> reshape its options, <b>Alignment</b> moves them
        away from its top edge, the <b>Line</b> indicator runs down the leading edge of the selected option
        instead of along its bottom, <b>Scrollable</b> scrolls the rail down its own length, and the header
        and the footer sit above and below the options.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div>Basic:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar TItem="BitNavBarOption" Vertical FitWidth>
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Home" IconName="@BitIconName.Home" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Products" IconName="@BitIconName.ProductVariant" />
