            <BitNavBarOption Text="Products" IconName="@BitIconName.ProductVariant" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Academy" IconName="@BitIconName.LearningTools" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Profile" IconName="@BitIconName.Contact" />
        </BitNavBar>
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    Clicked item: @eventsClickedOption?.Text
    <br />
    Selected item: @eventsSelectedOption?.Text
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Binding" RazorCode="@example18RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example18CsharpCode" Id="example18">
    <div>
        <b>SelectedItem</b> binds both ways, so the selection can be read and written from outside the navbar
        at any time. An option is captured with <b>@@ref</b> and handed to the binding, which is how the
        options API sets a selection of its own.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div>
        <b>DefaultSelectedItem</b>, which the items and the custom APIs open on, takes an item that already
        exists when the navbar reads its parameters, and an option only exists once it has rendered, which
        is after that. An options navbar therefore starts on a selection the same way it changes one: the
        captured reference is assigned to the bound selection on the first render.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div>Initial selection:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar TItem="BitNavBarOption" Mode="BitNavMode.Manual" @bind-SelectedItem="bindingSelectedOption">
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Home" IconName="@BitIconName.Home" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Products" IconName="@BitIconName.ProductVariant" @ref="bindingOptionProducts" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Academy" IconName="@BitIconName.LearningTools" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Profile" IconName="@BitIconName.Contact" />
        </BitNavBar>
    </div>
    <br />
    Selected item: @bindingSelectedOption?.Text
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>Two-way SelectedItem:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar TItem="BitNavBarOption" Mode="BitNavMode.Manual" @bind-SelectedItem="twoWaySelectedOption">
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Home" IconName="@BitIconName.Home" @ref="optionHome" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Products" IconName="@BitIconName.ProductVariant" @ref="optionProducts" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Academy" IconName="@BitIconName.LearningTools" @ref="optionAcademy" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Profile" IconName="@BitIconName.Contact" @ref="optionProfile" />
        </BitNavBar>
    </div>
    <br />
    Selected item: @twoWaySelectedOption?.Text
    <br /><br />
    <BitChoiceGroup Horizontal TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<BitNavBarOption>" TValue="BitNavBarOption" @bind-Value="@twoWaySelectedOption">
        <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Home" Id="Home" Value="optionHome" />
        <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Products" Id="Products" Value="optionProducts" />
        <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Academy" Id="Academy" Value="optionAcademy" />
        <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Profile" Id="Profile" Value="optionProfile" />
    </BitChoiceGroup>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Reselectable" RazorCode="@example19RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example19CsharpCode" Id="example19">
    <div>
        The events of an option that is already selected are swallowed, so a second click on the current
        destination changes nothing. <b>Reselectable</b> lets them through, which is what a bar that scrolls
        its page back to the top on a repeated click needs.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <BitToggle @bind-Value="reselectable" OnText="Enabled recalling" OffText="Disabled recalling" />
    <br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar TItem="BitNavBarOption" Mode="BitNavMode.Manual" OnItemClick="(BitNavBarOption option) => countClick++" Reselectable="reselectable">
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Home" IconName="@BitIconName.Home" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Products" IconName="@BitIconName.ProductVariant" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Academy" IconName="@BitIconName.LearningTools" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Profile" IconName="@BitIconName.Contact" />
        </BitNavBar>
    </div>
    <br />
    Item click count: @countClick
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Keyboard" RazorCode="@example20RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example20CsharpCode" Id="example20" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        By default the navbar is a set of links that <b>Tab</b> reaches one by one, which is how a navigation
        landmark behaves. <b>SingleTabStop</b> turns it into the roving tab index of a toolbar instead: the
        whole bar is a single stop that lands on the selected option, and the rest are reached from there.
        In both modes the arrow keys of either axis, <b>Home</b> and <b>End</b> move the focus along the bar,
        the horizontal pair flipping in RTL, and a disabled option is skipped. The focus stops at both ends;
        <b>WrapNavigation</b> lets it wrap around from the last option to the first one and back, and
        <b>SelectOnFocus</b> moves the selection along with the focus in the <b>Manual</b> mode, the way the
        tabs of a tab list do.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div>SingleTabStop:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar TItem="BitNavBarOption" SingleTabStop Mode="BitNavMode.Manual" @bind-SelectedItem="tabStopSelectedOption">
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Home" IconName="@BitIconName.Home" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Products" IconName="@BitIconName.ProductVariant" @ref="tabStopOptionProducts" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Academy" IconName="@BitIconName.LearningTools" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Profile" IconName="@BitIconName.Contact" />
        </BitNavBar>
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>WrapNavigation:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar TItem="BitNavBarOption" WrapNavigation SingleTabStop Mode="BitNavMode.Manual" @bind-SelectedItem="wrapSelectedOption">
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Home" IconName="@BitIconName.Home" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Products" IconName="@BitIconName.ProductVariant" @ref="wrapOptionProducts" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Academy" IconName="@BitIconName.LearningTools" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Profile" IconName="@BitIconName.Contact" />
        </BitNavBar>
    </div>
    <br /><br /><br /><br />
    <div>SelectOnFocus:</div><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar TItem="BitNavBarOption" SelectOnFocus SingleTabStop Mode="BitNavMode.Manual" @bind-SelectedItem="selectFocusSelectedOption">
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Home" IconName="@BitIconName.Home" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Products" IconName="@BitIconName.ProductVariant" @ref="selectFocusOptionProducts" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Academy" IconName="@BitIconName.LearningTools" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Profile" IconName="@BitIconName.Contact" />
        </BitNavBar>
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Selected Icon" RazorCode="@example21RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example21CsharpCode" Id="example21" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>
        A navigation bar conventionally marks its current destination with the filled variant of the same
        glyph, which is what <b>SelectedIconName</b> (and <b>SelectedIcon</b>, for an external icon library)
        is for: the option swaps to it while it is the selected one and falls back to its <b>IconName</b>
        otherwise. It reads as a second, shape-based cue for the selection next to the color, which is what
        keeps the current destination legible where a color difference alone is not enough. <b>Indicator</b>
        adds a third cue, drawn beside the content of the item rather than in it.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar TItem="BitNavBarOption" Mode="BitNavMode.Manual" @bind-SelectedItem="selectedIconSelectedOption">
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Home" IconName="@BitIconName.Home" SelectedIconName="@BitIconName.HomeSolid" @ref="selectedIconOptionHome" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Inbox" IconName="@BitIconName.Mail" SelectedIconName="@BitIconName.MailSolid" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Alerts" IconName="@BitIconName.Ringer" SelectedIconName="@BitIconName.RingerSolid" />
            <BitNavBarOption Text="Favorites" IconName="@BitIconName.Heart" SelectedIconName="@BitIconName.HeartFill" />
        </BitNavBar>
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Dynamic Items" RazorCode="@example22RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example22CsharpCode" Id="example22">
    <div>
        The options are rendered from a collection of their own, so options appended to (or removed from)
        the very same list are picked up without handing the navbar anything new. The selection survives as
        long as the option it points at is still in the list, and the keyboard follows the new order at once.
    </div>
    <br />
    <div>
        An option registers itself with the navbar as it renders, so one that appears after the first render
        lands at the end of that list wherever in the markup it sits, and reordering the markup does not move
        it at all: the options are still drawn in their own places, but the order the arrow keys, <b>Home</b>
        and <b>End</b> follow is the order they registered in. <b>AutoReorderOptions</b> reads the DOM order
        back after each render and puts the list in the order the options are written in, at the cost of one
        interop call per change. Reverse the items below and walk the bar with the arrow keys to tell the
        two apart.
    </div>
    <br /><br />
    <BitStack Horizontal>
        <BitButton OnClick="AddDynamicOption">Add item</BitButton>
        <BitButton OnClick="RemoveDynamicOption">Remove item</BitButton>
        <BitButton OnClick="ReverseDynamicOptions">Reverse items</BitButton>
    </BitStack>
    <br />
    <BitToggle @bind-Value="dynamicAutoReorder" OnText="AutoReorderOptions" OffText="AutoReorderOptions" />
    <br />
    <div class="container">
        <BitNavBar TItem="BitNavBarOption"
                   Mode="BitNavMode.Manual"
                   AutoReorderOptions="dynamicAutoReorder"
                   @bind-SelectedItem="dynamicSelectedOption">
            @* The key ties each option to its entry in the collection, so reversing the list moves the very
               same options around instead of leaving them in place with new texts on them - which is what
               keeps the selection on the option it was on, and what leaves the registration order behind
               for AutoReorderOptions to put right. *@
            @foreach (var option in dynamicOptions)
            {
                <BitNavBarOption @key="option" Text="@option.Text" IconName="@option.IconName" />
            }
        </BitNavBar>
    </div>
    <br />
    Selected item: @dynamicSelectedOption?.Text
</DemoExample>

